 = -0.12). Room preparation, meeting requests, price–performance balance, proactive problem identification, and food hygiene did not differ significantly at the nominal level.

Twelve items had
p &lt;
0.001 (Figure 1) and therefore meet the Bonferroni threshold of 0.002273. Six were negative gaps—Items 2, 3, 4, 16, 17, and 18—and six were positive—Items 1, 8, 10, 11, 13, and 15. The six principal shortfalls therefore remain supported under this conservative familywise check.

Items 7, 9, 14, and 20 had
p &lt;
0.01, while Item 19 had
p &lt;
0.05. These thresholds alone do not establish whether the items meet the Bonferroni criterion. The familywise result is therefore expressed as a lower bound of 12 items, including all six principal negative gaps.

The SERVQUAL–IPA assessment identified six principal perceived shortfalls at the studied hotel: common-area cleanliness, room cleanliness, equipment functionality, reservation accuracy, personal-data protection, and transaction security. All six had
p &lt;
0.001 and met the conservative Bonferroni criterion for 22 comparisons.
